course: biology
lesson: 255
title: Plants deeper: poisonous and stinging plants and staying safe
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A plant cannot run away from a hungry animal, so many plants defend themselves with stings, spines, burning juices and poisons.
explain: Brush your leg against a nettle and you feel it at once: its hairs are tiny glass-like needles that snap off and push an itchy, burning mix into your skin.
explain: Some poisons are much more serious, which is why the golden rule is simple: never eat berries, leaves or mushrooms you find unless an adult who really knows says they are safe.
explain: Poison and medicine are often the same thing in different amounts: foxgloves are poisonous, yet a medicine made from them has helped people with heart problems for over 200 years.
explain: The poisoned apple in Snow White is a fairy tale; a real apple is safe, and the few pips you swallow by accident pass straight through.
fact: Nettle hairs are tiny glass-like needles that break off and inject an irritating mix into the skin, which protects the leaves from grazing animals; the sting hurts and itches but wears off by itself.
fact: Cooked nettles lose their sting and are safe to eat in soup; rubbing with a dock leaf may soothe a sting, partly just because rubbing helps.
fact: Many berries are poisonous to people even though birds can eat them, because birds' bodies handle some poisons differently.
fact: Deadly nightshade has tempting shiny black berries and a poison that affects the nerves and heart; its other name, belladonna, means «beautiful lady», because women once put drops in their eyes to make the pupils big.
fact: Poison ivy, found in North America, has an oil that gives most people an itchy rash; the rule is «leaves of three, let it be».
fact: Poison ivy oil can stay on clothes and dogs' fur and give a rash later, and smoke from burning it is dangerous for the lungs.
fact: Giant hogweed can grow taller than a grown-up, and its sap makes skin burn and blister in sunlight, so it must never be touched.
fact: Foxgloves are poisonous, but the medicine digitalis made from them has helped heart patients since the doctor William Withering wrote about it in 1785.
fact: Castor beans hold one of the strongest plant poisons, but castor oil is safe after processing and is used in medicine, soap and machine oil.
fact: Rhubarb stalks are safe to eat cooked, but rhubarb leaves are poisonous because they contain a lot of oxalic acid.
fact: Chillies contain capsaicin, which tricks pain sensors into feeling heat; mammals avoid chillies, but birds cannot feel the burn and spread the seeds.
fact: Milk calms a chilli burn better than water does.
fact: Cactus spines are leaves turned into spikes that guard the water inside and give a little shade.
fact: Apple seeds contain a substance that can make a poison only if very many are crushed and eaten; a few swallowed whole pass through harmlessly.
exercise: Why does a nettle sting? | Its tiny needle-like hairs break and inject an itchy mix | It bites with rows of tiny sharp teeth hidden under its leaves | It sprays acid out of its roots | Its leaves are red hot in the sun
exercise: Why do nettles have stinging hairs? | To stop grazing animals eating the leaves | To catch insects for food on their sticky leaves and stems | To keep their leaves warm | To help them climb up walls
exercise: What happens to nettles when they are cooked? | They lose their sting and are safe in soup | They sting even more strongly once they have been boiled | They turn into a poison | They grow new hairs at once
exercise: What should you do about red berries on a bush in the park? | Never eat them unless an adult who really knows says they are safe | Eat them if you can see birds eating them, because then they are safe | Taste one to see if it is sweet | Eat them only if they are very red
exercise: Why can birds eat some berries that are poisonous to us? | Their bodies handle the poison differently | Birds never swallow the berries | Birds wash the berries first | The berries are only poisonous in summer
exercise: Why is deadly nightshade also called belladonna? | Women once dropped it in their eyes to make the pupils big | It grows only in the gardens of rich ladies in Italy | Its flowers look like a lady's dress | It was named after a famous queen
exercise: What is the rule to remember about poison ivy? | Leaves of three, let it be | Red and yellow, kill a fellow | Shiny and green, safe to be seen | Spiky and tall, touch it all
exercise: Why should you wash clothes and the dog after a hike near poison ivy? | Its oil can stay on them and give a rash later | Poison ivy seeds stick to clothes and grow on them in the wash | The dog may turn into poison ivy | The mud from hikes is always poisonous
exercise: What makes giant hogweed so dangerous? | Its sap makes skin burn and blister in sunlight | Its leaves explode with a bang when anybody touches them | It grows so fast it traps people | Its flowers spray stinging dust in the dark
exercise: Which medicine comes from the foxglove? | Digitalis, for heart problems | Penicillin, for infections | Aspirin, for headaches | Insulin, for diabetes
exercise: Which part of the rhubarb plant is poisonous? | The leaves | The stalks | The pink skin | The cooked crumble
exercise: What does capsaicin in chillies do? | It tricks pain sensors into feeling heat | It really heats your mouth up to boiling point | It burns a small hole in your tongue | It makes your teeth hot
exercise: What helps calm a chilli burn best? | Milk | Cold water | Fizzy drink | Hot tea
exercise: What are cactus spines? | Leaves turned into spikes | Hairs that grow from the roots | Seeds waiting to fall off | Thorns glued on by birds
exercise: What happens if you swallow a few apple pips by accident? | They pass through harmlessly | You fall into a deep sleep | You must go to hospital at once | An apple tree grows in your tummy
exercise: Which is safe to use after processing, though its bean is very poisonous? | Castor oil | Rhubarb leaf tea | Deadly nightshade drops | Hogweed sap
test: In Snow White the queen gives her a poisoned apple that sends her to sleep. What is true about real apples? | Apples are safe; only many crushed seeds could ever make a poison | All apples are a little bit poisonous, so you should eat only one a week | Red apples are poisonous and green ones safe | Apple peel is the poisonous part
test: «my mate says chillies actually burn ur mouth like fire, it's real heat» What is right? | No, capsaicin only tricks the pain sensors into feeling heat | Yes, chillies really heat your mouth up to about 100 degrees, like a kettle | Yes, that is why chillies are red | No, chillies are cold inside
test: «touched a nettle, ffs it hurts, am i gonna die» What is right? | No, the sting hurts and itches but wears off by itself | Yes, nettle stings are deadly within an hour | Yes, unless you drink a big glass of cold milk at once, straight away | No, but the leg will stay red for ever
test: A family burns a pile of poison ivy in the garden. Why is that a bad idea? | The smoke carries the oil and is dangerous for the lungs | The fire will spread the poison ivy seeds everywhere in the wind | It makes the poison ivy grow back faster | Burning poison ivy makes a loud explosion
test: «birds are eating them berries so they're fine for us, right» What is right? | No, birds can eat some berries that are poisonous to people | Yes, if a bird eats it, it is safe for people to eat too | Yes, but only the red ones | No, birds only eat poisonous berries
test: How can the same foxglove be both a poison and a medicine? | The amount decides: a careful dose helps, too much harms | It is poison in summer and medicine in winter | The flowers are medicine and the stalks poison | Only doctors are safe from its poison
test: What would happen if a child touched giant hogweed sap and then played in the sun? | The skin could burn and blister badly | The skin would go green for a day | Nothing, the sap only works in the dark | The child would fall asleep
test: Count them: on a hike Sam spots 4 poison ivy leaves, each one «leaves of three». How many leaflets is that? | 12 | 7 | 16 | 3
test: Why would a desert cactus need to guard itself? | Its stored water would tempt thirsty animals | Cacti are afraid of the dark and the spines keep them company | Desert insects eat only spines | It needs spines to drink the rain
test: Grandma makes rhubarb crumble. Is it safe? | Yes, if she uses only the stalks and not the leaves | No, rhubarb is poisonous all over, stalks and leaves alike | Yes, the leaves are the tastiest part | No, rhubarb is only safe raw
test: Why might a chilli plant «want» birds but not mammals to eat its fruit? | Birds feel no burn and spread the seeds whole | Birds are smaller, so they eat less | Mammals cannot reach the fruit anyway | Birds give the plant water in return
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a person ought not to consume berries found growing wild. | Many such berries are poisonous to human beings, notwithstanding their consumption by birds | All wild berries are invariably poisonous | Berries become poisonous upon being touched | Wild berries contain no nourishment whatsoever
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the sensation produced by the consumption of chillies. | The substance capsaicin stimulates pain receptors so as to produce a sensation of heat | The temperature of the mouth is raised substantially, often to the point of scalding | The tongue is chemically burnt and permanently damaged | The sensation arises from the colour of the fruit
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the relationship between the foxglove and the medicine digitalis? | A substance poisonous in large quantity is beneficial to the heart in a carefully regulated dose | The foxglove is entirely harmless in any quantity | Digitalis is a remedy against the sting of nettles | The foxglove was discovered to be poisonous only in recent years, after it had long been eaten as a salad
exam: Ascertain which part of the rhubarb plant is unsuitable for human consumption. | The leaves, by reason of their high content of oxalic acid | The stalks, whether cooked or raw, at any season | The roots, by reason of their sweetness, which attracts children | No part of the plant whatsoever
